PRIVATE REFLECTION: {All 8 colleagues converged on B with identical core reasoning: poly(dT) capture + 75×225 sequencing is strictly 3′-biased and cannot reach CDR3 (located hundreds of nt upstream in the V(D)J segment). Inserting a TSO immediately after the UMI enables template-switching during RT, appending a 5′ primer site so that the subsequent long read spans the entire V(D)J junction while preserving bead chemistry, cell-label/UMI, and the inexpensive sequencing format.  
A/C rejected because they require redesigning capture oligos on the beads (violates “keep the same bead schematic”). D rejected because it assumes CDR3 is already on the captured molecule. E rejected because it violates the explicit cost/sequencing constraint.  
One colleague notes that BD Rhapsody’s own immune-receptor protocol already implements an analogous TSO-based 5′ workflow, confirming compatibility. No disagreements or errors observed. Current consensus is therefore B.}

